🚨 severity: BLOCKING —
🚨 **BLOCKING: CRAP ratchet failure (red CI) — `removeStaleAppendix` measured CRAP 10 > threshold 8**

The rework hunks that fixed the `file_delete` contract (commit `3881e5ec6`) pushed this function over the repo's CRAP ratchet. Both failed `crap` checks on this head report the same measurement (ci_failures.md, `crap` job — runs 43191214482/43191091107):

```
CRAP Ratchet
============
NEW > 8  js/common/developmentModeAdaptive.js::removeStaleAppendix: 10
0 regression(s), 1 new over threshold, 0 improvement(s), 0 removed
##[error]Process completed with exit code 2.
```

The unit suite itself is green (9799 passed, 0 failed in the same job) — the break is pure complexity growth: the existence probe + two try/catch blocks + the compound `if (existed && !(result && result.deleted === true))` all landed in one function (CC 10 at 100% coverage ⇒ CRAP 10).

**Fix — decompose into helpers, the exact discipline this PR's sibling module already documents** (`js/common/developmentModePlan.js` `appendixOverridePath`: split so every function stays under the CRAP threshold; the same file's `thresholdSetting`/`resolveThresholds` follow it). Keep the probe BEFORE the delete (reading after deletion would always report "missing"):

```javascript
/** Readability probe: true when the appendix file exists (unreadable ⇒ assume stale). */
function staleAppendixExists(io) {
    var read = (io && io.file_read) || file_read;
    try {
        return typeof read({ path: developmentModePlan.PLAN_APPENDIX_FILE_PATH }) === 'string';
    } catch (e) {
        return true;
    }
}

/** Delete the appendix, contained — the platform returns {deleted:bool} and never throws. */
function deleteStaleAppendix(io) {
    var remove = (io && io.file_delete) || file_delete;
    try {
        return remove({ path: developmentModePlan.PLAN_APPENDIX_FILE_PATH });
    } catch (e) {
        return null; // defensive only — the platform does not throw
    }
}

function removeStaleAppendix(io) {
    var existed = staleAppendixExists(io);
    var result = deleteStaleAppendix(io);
    if (existed && !(result && result.deleted === true)) {
        console.warn('Could not remove the stale plan-mode appendix at "' +
            developmentModePlan.PLAN_APPENDIX_FILE_PATH + '": ' +
            developmentMode.describeRawValue(result));
    }
}
```

Behavior, tests and the documented warning contract stay byte-identical — only the CRAP number crosses back under the ratchet (the existing three failure-shape tests pin all three helpers' paths).

*This does not re-open the resolved `file_delete` thread — the fix itself is correct and stays; only its complexity is over the measured gate.*

⚠️ severity: IMPORTANT —
⚠️ **IMPORTANT: `removeStaleAppendix` never detects a failed delete — dmtools `file_delete` signals failure via the return object, not by throwing**

Verified empirically against the installed dmtools runtime (probe script via `dmtools run`):

```json
{"nonexistent":{"deleted":false},"real":{"deleted":true}}
```

`file_delete` **never throws** — it returns `{deleted: false}` for a missing path and `{deleted: true}` on success. This code (and the test at `test_developmentModeAdaptive.js:565`, which simulates failure by throwing) assume the opposite contract, so the `catch` below is dead code and the documented "one honest warning on a deletion failure" (`js/common/developmentModeAdaptive.js:388-390`) can never fire:

```javascript
try {
    remove({ path: developmentModePlan.PLAN_APPENDIX_FILE_PATH });
} catch (e) {
    console.warn('Could not remove the stale plan-mode appendix at "' + ... + '":', e);
}
```

**Why it matters:** on a reused workspace where a previous leg materialized the appendix, a routed-to-**default** adaptive leg relies entirely on this cleanup (the condition script `isDevelopmentModePlan.js` then probes the file — a surviving stale file means the plan-protocol appendix rides a routed-default prompt). That directly violates this PR's own invariant (`docs/development-mode.md` §2.3: "a routed-to-default leg and any shadow leg remove stale appendix state, so their prompts stay byte-identical to `default`") and AC4. Shadow legs are safe (the condition script's zero-read guard is the second line of defense there), but routed-default legs have no backstop.

**Suggested fix** — consult the return object, and only warn when a file actually existed (a plain `{deleted:false}` is the normal clean-workspace response and must stay quiet):

```javascript
function removeStaleAppendix(io) {
    var remove = (io && io.file_delete) || file_delete;
    var existed = typeof read({ path: developmentModePlan.PLAN_APPENDIX_FILE_PATH }) === 'string';
    var result = null;
    try {
        result = remove({ path: developmentModePlan.PLAN_APPENDIX_FILE_PATH });
    } catch (e) {
        result = null; // defensive only — the platform does not throw
    }
    if (existed && !(result && result.deleted === true)) {
        console.warn('Could not remove the stale plan-mode appendix at "' +
            developmentModePlan.PLAN_APPENDIX_FILE_PATH + '":', result);
    }
}
```

and update `fsRecorder`'s failure test (`test_developmentModeAdaptive.js:565`) to simulate the real failure shape (`file_delete` returning `{deleted:false}` after an existence probe) instead of a throw.

